          "local_context": "“Deploying weapons in orbit.” Wow, that sounds like a powerful headline. But when we talk about space, important differences disappear if we put everything in the same category. Let’s quickly sort things out.\n\nOn September 14, 2026, the U.S. Secretary of the Air Force said that the United States is deploying space control weapons in orbit to protect the joint force from actions by hostile parties. An official U.S. government article records this as the first statement confirming that the Space Force has deployed weapons in space. So far, only the fact of deployment is confirmed. The specific system names, attack capabilities, and targets are not confirmed.\n\nWhat about ground-launched ASATs? In November 2021, Russia destroyed a satellite with a ground-launched anti-satellite missile that flew directly to it, creating more than 1,500 pieces of trackable debris. This was a test launched from the ground, so it is a different category from permanently placing weapons in orbit. Even when the subject is military activity in space, we need to sort these things out.",
