          "detected_by": "stage1_llm",
          "local_context": "Next is “counterspace.” In the U.S. Space Force’s framework, this is a broad term for attacks and defenses carried out in orbit, through communication links, and in ground-based parts of the system. It includes defense as well as attack. Defensive measures include warnings about threats, making equipment harder to damage, separating functions, mobility, and backup systems. These protective measures are a different category from deploying weapons for attacks in orbit.\n\nThen there is the line drawn by the Outer Space Treaty. It bans placing objects carrying nuclear weapons or other weapons of mass destruction in orbit around Earth, putting such weapons on the Moon or other celestial bodies, and deploying them in outer space in other ways. It also bans military bases and fortifications on celestial bodies, as well as weapons tests and military exercises there. The key is not to lump everything together under the phrase “space weapons,” but to look at the type of weapon, its location, and the action involved.\n\nIf this were about protecting your own daily life, would you put warnings and tools for attack in the same category? With space news, too, don’t decide based only on how powerful the words sound. First, sort things out. Which boundary concerns you most?",
